
## Hyperosmolar Therapy in TBI: Mannitol vs Hypertonic Saline

Both mannitol and hypertonic saline (HTS) are first-line hyperosmolar agents for managing elevated intracranial pressure (ICP) in traumatic brain injury (TBI), but they differ substantially in mechanism, hemodynamic effects, safety profile, and practical ICU considerations. [^1_1][^1_2][^1_3] Current evidence shows comparable effects on mortality and long-term neurological outcomes, with HTS demonstrating more sustained ICP reduction, better cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP), and fewer treatment failures in several meta-analyses. [^1_1][^1_4][^1_5][^1_6]

Below are structured comparisons across multiple domains.

***

## 1. Mechanism of Action

| Feature | Mannitol | Hypertonic Saline (HTS) |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| **Primary mechanism** | Osmotic diuretic: creates osmotic gradient across intact BBB, pulling water from brain → urine excretion [^1_2] | Osmotic agent + volume expander: increases serum Na⁺/osmolality, draws water out of brain; also expands intravascular volume [^1_2][^1_3] |
| **Onset of ICP reduction** | 10–20 minutes | 10–20 minutes [^1_5] |
| **Peak effect** | 30–60 minutes | 30–60 minutes [^1_5] |
| **Duration of effect** | ~1.5–3 hours (shorter) | ~3–6 hours (longer) [^1_7][^1_8] |
| **Effect on blood viscosity** | Reduces viscosity → improves microcirculatory flow (rheologic effect) [^1_3] | Minimal rheologic effect; primarily osmotic + hemodynamic |
| **BBB dependency** | Requires relatively intact BBB; may accumulate with BBB disruption → rebound ICP [^1_3] | Works even with some BBB disruption; less rebound risk [^1_9] |


***

## 2. Hemodynamic and Renal Effects

| Parameter | Mannitol | Hypertonic Saline |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| **Intravascular volume** | Initial expansion → diuresis → net volume depletion; risk of hypovolemia/hypotension [^1_2][^1_3] | Volume expansion; improves preload and MAP; beneficial in hypotensive/hemodynamically unstable patients [^1_2][^1_8] |
| **Blood pressure** | May decrease (due to diuresis) | May increase (volume expansion) [^1_1] |
| **Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP)** | Modest increase | Greater and more sustained CPP increase (meta-analysis: +5.5 to +9 mmHg at 30–120 min) [^1_1][^1_8] |
| **Renal effects** | Osmotic nephrosis risk; AKI with cumulative doses, especially >200 g/day or serum osmolality >320 mOsm/kg [^1_3] | Generally safer for kidneys; hypernatremia risk but less direct nephrotoxicity [^1_10] |
| **Electrolyte disturbances** | Hyponatremia (dilutional), hyperkalemia possible | Hypernatremia, hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis (with large volumes of 3% or 23.4%) [^1_9][^1_3] |
| **Serum osmolality target** | Keep <320 mOsm/kg to avoid AKI [^1_3] | No strict upper limit; monitor Na⁺ (goal often 145–155 mEq/L for ICP control) [^1_9] |


***

## 3. Efficacy: ICP Control and Clinical Outcomes

| Outcome | Mannitol | Hypertonic Saline | Evidence |
| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |
| **ICP reduction (immediate, 30–60 min)** | Effective | Similar or slightly better | No significant difference at 30–60 min in meta-analysis [^1_1][^1_5] |
| **ICP reduction (sustained, 90–120 min)** | Less sustained | Significantly greater reduction (MD −2.33 mmHg; P<0.00001) [^1_1] | [^1_1][^1_5][^1_8] |
| **ICP treatment failure** | Higher failure rate | Lower failure rate (RR 0.39; 95% CI 0.18–0.81) [^1_6] | [^1_6] |
| **Mortality** | No significant difference | Trend toward lower mortality (RR 0.69; 95% CI 0.45–1.04; P=0.08) [^1_1] | [^1_1][^1_7][^1_11] |
| **Favorable neurological outcome (GOS-E/GOSE)** | No difference | No difference (RR 1.28; 95% CI 0.86–1.90; P=0.23) [^1_1] | [^1_1][^1_7][^1_9][^1_11] |
| **ICU length of stay** | Longer | Shorter by ~1.2 days (MD −1.18; P<0.01) [^1_7] | [^1_7] |
| **Combined ICP+CPP burden** | Higher burden | Significantly lower days with ICP↑ + CPP↓ (0.6 vs 2.4 days; P<0.01) [^1_4] | [^1_4] |

**Key point:** HTS shows superior sustained ICP control, better CPP, and fewer treatment failures, but no clear mortality or functional outcome advantage in current RCTs/meta-analyses. [^1_1][^1_4][^1_6]

***

## 4. Dosing and Administration

| Aspect | Mannitol | Hypertonic Saline |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| **Common concentrations** | 20% (200 mg/mL) | 3%, 7.5%, 23.4% |
| **Typical bolus dose** | 0.25–1 g/kg IV over 10–20 min [^1_3] | 3%: 2–5 mL/kg; 23.4%: 30 mL bolus (central line) [^1_2] |
| **Continuous infusion** | Not recommended | 3% infusion (0.5–1 mL/kg/h) for sustained ICP control [^1_2] |
| **Monitoring** | Serum osmolality, creatinine, urine output, electrolytes | Serum Na⁺, osmolality, volume status, acid-base [^1_9][^1_3] |
| **Cumulative dose limits** | Avoid >200 g/day or osmolality >320 mOsm/kg [^1_3] | No strict limit; avoid Na⁺ >160 mEq/L acutely |
| **Rebound ICP risk** | Higher (especially with BBB disruption, repeated dosing) [^1_3] | Lower [^1_9] |


***

## 5. Adverse Effects and Contraindications

| Complication | Mannitol | Hypertonic Saline |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| **Hypovolemia/hypotension** | Common (osmotic diuresis) [^1_2][^1_3] | Rare; may cause volume overload [^1_3] |
| **Acute kidney injury (AKI)** | Well-documented; dose-dependent [^1_10][^1_3] | Less nephrotoxic; combination with mannitol does not increase AKI risk in young TBI patients [^1_10] |
| **Electrolyte abnormalities** | Hyponatremia, hyperkalemia | Hypernatremia, hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis [^1_9][^1_3] |
| **Rebound ICP** | Significant risk with BBB disruption, cumulative dosing [^1_3] | Minimal |
| **Central pontine myelinolysis (CPM)** | Rare | Theoretical risk with rapid Na⁺ correction; avoid Na⁺ rise >10–12 mEq/L/24h [^1_3] |
| **Pulmonary edema** | Rare (unless volume overloaded pre-dose) | Possible with large-volume 3% infusions in heart failure [^1_3] |
| **Contraindications** | Severe renal failure, anuria, severe dehydration, serum osmolality >320 mOsm/kg [^1_3] | Severe hypernatremia, uncontrolled heart failure, severe metabolic acidosis (relative) |


***

## 6. Evidence Summary: Key Trials and Meta-Analyses

| Study / Meta-analysis | Design | Population | Key Findings |
| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |
| **Burgess et al. (2016)** [^1_6] | Systematic review (7 RCTs, 191 pts) | Severe TBI | No difference in mortality/neurological outcome; HTS had fewer ICP treatment failures (RR 0.39) |
| **Li et al. (2019)** [^1_1] | Meta-analysis (12 RCTs, 464 pts) | TBI with raised ICP | HTS: lower ICP at 90–120 min (−2.33 mmHg), higher CPP (+5.5 to +9 mmHg); trend to lower mortality (RR 0.69; P=0.08); no difference in favorable outcome |
| **Wang et al. (2019)** [^1_9] | Meta-analysis | Severe TBI | HS more effective for successful ICP control (RR 1.06; P=0.044); no difference in mortality/function; HS preferred for refractory ICP |
| **Sahni et al. (2020)** [^1_4] | Observational cohort | Severe TBI | HTS superior for combined ICP↑+CPP↓ burden (0.6 vs 2.4 days; P<0.01) |
| **SOS Trial (protocol, 2020–2022)** [^1_12][^1_13] | UK multicentre RCT (ongoing/completed) | Severe TBI with ICP monitoring | Primary outcome: GOSE at 6 months; designed to definitively compare HTS vs mannitol (results pending) |
| **Pediatric TBI (JAMA Netw Open 2025)** [^1_14] | Observational (518 children) | Severe pediatric TBI | No difference in survival or functional outcomes; HTS better ICP/CPP reduction [^1_15] |
| **Combination therapy (MHS vs HS)** [^1_10] | Observational | TBI patients | Mannitol + HTS did not increase AKI risk vs HTS alone in younger patients |

**Guideline position:** Brain Trauma Foundation 4th edition states insufficient evidence to recommend one agent over the other; choice should be individualized. [^1_8]

***

## 7. Practical ICU Considerations

| Scenario | Preferred Agent | Rationale |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| **Hemodynamically unstable / hypotensive TBI** | HTS (3% or 23.4%) | Volume expansion, improves MAP/CPP; avoids mannitol-induced hypovolemia [^1_2][^1_8] |
| **Renal impairment / AKI risk** | HTS | Mannitol contraindicated in severe renal failure; HTS safer [^1_10][^1_3] |
| **Refractory ICP after mannitol** | HTS | HTS effective after mannitol failure; longer duration of action [^1_2][^1_9] |
| **Severe hypernatremia (Na⁺ >155–160)** | Mannitol | Avoid worsening hypernatremia with HTS |
| **Serum osmolality >320 mOsm/kg** | HTS | Mannitol contraindicated; HTS does not add to osmolal gap in same way [^1_3] |
| **Need for sustained ICP control** | HTS infusion | Continuous 3% infusion provides stable ICP control; longer duration per dose [^1_2][^1_8] |
| **Resource-limited settings** | Mannitol | Cheaper, widely available; HTS (especially 23.4%) may be costly or unavailable [^1_8] |
| **Combination therapy** | Both (sequential or alternating) | May be used in refractory ICP; no increased AKI risk in young patients [^1_10] |


***

## 8. Cost and Availability

| Factor | Mannitol | Hypertonic Saline |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| **Cost** | Low; generic, widely available | 3%: moderate; 23.4%: expensive [^1_8] |
| **Availability** | Universal | 23.4% may require pharmacy compounding; not universally stocked |
| **Storage** | Room temperature | 23.4% requires careful handling (vesicant; central line preferred) |


