## 11. Common Exam/Bedside Pearls

| ⚠ Pitfall | 💡 Pearl |
| :-- | :-- |
| Giving repeated mannitol without checking osmolality → AKI, rebound ICP | Check serum osmolality q6–12h; keep <320 mOsm/kg with mannitol [^1_3] |
| Using mannitol in hypotensive patient → worsens hypotension/CPP | Choose HTS in hypotension; it expands volume and improves CPP [^1_2][^1_8] |
| Rapid Na⁺ correction with HTS → CPM risk | Limit Na⁺ rise to ≤10–12 mEq/L/24h; monitor q4–6h initially [^1_3] |
| Assuming HTS always superior for outcomes | Evidence shows better ICP/CPP control, but no proven mortality/functional benefit yet [^1_1][^1_11] |
| Not using HTS after mannitol failure | HTS effective in refractory ICP; switch or alternate [^1_2][^1_9] |


***

## Topic Pearls

- **ICP control:** HTS provides more sustained ICP reduction and higher CPP than mannitol, especially at 90–120 minutes post-dose. [^1_1][^1_5]
- **Outcomes:** No significant difference in mortality or favorable neurological outcome between HTS and mannitol in current meta-analyses. [^1_1][^1_11]
- **Hemodynamics:** HTS is a volume expander (beneficial in hypotension); mannitol causes diuresis and may worsen hypovolemia. [^1_2][^1_3]
- **Renal safety:** Mannitol carries dose-dependent AKI risk (avoid if osmolality >320 mOsm/kg); HTS is safer in renal impairment. [^1_10][^1_3]
- **Rebound ICP:** Higher risk with mannitol, especially with BBB disruption and cumulative dosing. [^1_3]
- **Treatment failures:** HTS associated with fewer ICP treatment failures (RR ~0.39). [^1_6]
- **Guidelines:** Brain Trauma Foundation states insufficient evidence to prefer one agent; choice should be individualized based on hemodynamics, renal function, and electrolytes. [^1_8]

